from lessons import L
L(12, 'Functions: your own commands', '⚙️', 30,
  'Write your own functions, give them inputs (parameters), get answers back with return, and know where variables live.',
  ['I can write a function with def and call it', 'I can use parameters, default values and several arguments', 'I know return gives a value back and print only shows it', 'I know a variable made inside a function stays inside it'],
  [
  ('p', 'Imagine you want to print a birthday card message for ten friends. You could copy the same <code>print</code> lines ten times. Or you can write the steps <b>once</b>, give them a <b>name</b>, and use that name whenever you need them. That named recipe is a <b>function</b>. You already use functions: <code>print()</code>, <code>input()</code> and <code>int()</code> are all functions somebody else wrote.'),
  ('h', 'Making a function: def'),
  ('try', 'def greet():\n    print("Hello!")\n    print("Welcome to my program.")\n\ngreet()\ngreet()', [], 'Run it. Then add a third <code>greet()</code> line at the bottom and run again.'),
  ('rule', 'def + a name + brackets + colon + an indented body', 'Start with <code>def</code>, the name, <code>()</code> and a <b>colon</b>. Everything <b>indented</b> underneath is the <b>body</b>: the steps of the recipe. To use the function you <b>call</b> it by writing its name with brackets: <code>greet()</code>.'),
  ('trap', '<b>def does NOT run the function.</b> It only teaches Python the recipe. Nothing happens until a line <i>calls</i> it. And the brackets matter: <code>greet</code> alone does nothing, <code>greet()</code> runs it.'),
  ('try', 'def greet():\n    print("Hello!")\n\nprint("A")\ngreet\nprint("B")', [], 'Predict first: does "Hello!" show? Then run. Now add the brackets to <code>greet</code> and run again.'),
  ('p', 'Python reads from the top down, so <b>define first, call later</b>. Calling a function before its <code>def</code> has run is a NameError.'),
  ('h', 'Parameters: give the function something to work with'),
  ('p', 'A function that always does the same thing is not very useful. Put a variable name inside the brackets and the function can work with different values each time. That variable is a <b>parameter</b>. The value you pass in the call is the <b>argument</b>.'),
  ('try', 'def greet(name):\n    print("Hello,", name)\n\ngreet("Ana")\ngreet("Leo")\ngreet(input())', ['Mia'], 'Each call gives <code>name</code> a new value. Change the names and run.'),
  ('p', 'With several parameters, the arguments go in <b>order</b>: the first argument goes to the first parameter. You can also say the names yourself (<code>b=1, a=10</code>); then the order does not matter. You can give a parameter a <b>default value</b>, and then you may leave that argument out.'),
  ('show', 'def subtract(a, b):\n    print(a - b)\n\nsubtract(10, 4)\nsubtract(4, 10)\nsubtract(b=1, a=10)\n\ndef power(base, exp=2):\n    print(base ** exp)\n\npower(5)\npower(2, 3)', []),
  ('trap', 'The number of arguments must fit. <code>def add(a, b)</code> called as <code>add(1)</code> or <code>add(1, 2, 3)</code> is a <b>TypeError</b>. Python does not guess the missing value.'),
  ('h', 'return vs print - the big one'),
  ('p', '<code>print()</code> <b>shows</b> something on the screen. But often you want a function to <b>give an answer back</b> so the rest of the program can use it. That is what <code>return</code> does.'),
  ('show', 'def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nx = add(2, 3)\nprint(x)\nprint(add(10, 20) * 2)', []),
  ('trace', 'def add(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nx = add(2, 3)\ny = x * 10\nprint(y)', []),
  ('rule', 'return gives a value back; print only shows it', '<code>return a + b</code> sends the answer back to the line that called the function, where you can store it (<code>x = add(2, 3)</code>) or use it in more math. <b>return also ends the function</b>: any line after it never runs. A function with <b>no return</b> gives back <code>None</code>. So in a function that has <code>return "A"</code> on one line and <code>print("B")</code> on the next, the print never happens.'),
  ('try', 'def add_print(a, b):\n    print(a + b)\n\ndef add_return(a, b):\n    return a + b\n\nx = add_print(2, 3)\ny = add_return(2, 3)\nprint(x)\nprint(y)', [], 'Predict the 3 lines before you run. Which one shows None, and why?'),
  ('trap', '<b>print is not return.</b> A function that only prints gives back <code>None</code>. <code>add_print(2, 3) + 1</code> would crash with a TypeError, because None cannot be added to a number. If you want the answer to be used again, use <b>return</b>.'),
  ('h', 'Scope: where does a variable live?'),
  ('p', 'A variable made <b>inside</b> a function is <b>local</b>: it lives only while the function runs. Parameters are local too. Outside the function the name does not exist.'),
  ('try', 'def make():\n    secret = 42\n    print("inside:", secret)\n\nmake()\nprint("outside:", secret)', [], 'Run it and read the error. Which line crashed? Change the last line to <code>print("outside")</code> to fix it.'),
  ('p', 'A function may <b>read</b> a variable from outside (a global). But if the function <b>assigns</b> to that name, Python creates a brand new local variable that has nothing to do with the outside one.'),
  ('show', 'x = 10\n\ndef change():\n    x = 99\n    print("in change:", x)\n\nchange()\nprint("outside:", x)', []),
  ('trap', '<b>count += 1 inside a function</b> is an <b>UnboundLocalError</b> when <code>count</code> was made outside. The += assigns, so Python thinks <code>count</code> is local, and a local count has no value yet.'),
  ('rule', 'The easy way: pass in, return out', 'You could write <code>global count</code> as the first line of the function to say "I mean the outside variable". But it is cleaner to <b>pass the value in</b> and <b>return the new value</b>: <code>def add_one(n): return n + 1</code> and then <code>count = add_one(count)</code>.'),
  ('h', 'Functions can call functions (small helpers)'),
  ('show', 'def is_even(n):\n    return n % 2 == 0\n\ndef area(w, h):\n    return w * h\n\ndef describe(w, h):\n    a = area(w, h)\n    if is_even(a):\n        return "area " + str(a) + " is even"\n    return "area " + str(a) + " is odd"\n\nprint(describe(3, 4))\nprint(describe(3, 5))', []),
  ('table', ['Mistake', 'Error it causes'], [
     ['<code>hi()</code> on a line ABOVE <code>def hi():</code>', 'NameError'],
     ['<code>def hi()</code> with no colon', 'SyntaxError'],
     ['body not indented under the def', 'IndentationError'],
     ['<code>hi</code> instead of <code>hi()</code>', 'no error, but nothing runs'],
     ['Calling <code>add(1)</code> when add needs 2 arguments', 'TypeError'],
     ['Calling <code>add(1, 2, 3)</code> when add needs 2', 'TypeError'],
     ['Using a variable from inside a function, outside it', 'NameError'],
     ['<code>count += 1</code> inside a function with no global', 'UnboundLocalError'],
     ['<code>print("Sum: " + add(1, 2))</code> (a number, not text)', 'TypeError - convert with str() or use a comma'],
     ['<code>add(1, 2) + 1</code> when add only prints', 'TypeError - None + 1'],
     ['Calling <code>Hi()</code> when you defined <code>hi</code>', 'NameError - capital letters count']]),
  ('quick', 'A function has no return line. What does print(f()) show?', ['None', '0', 'An empty line', 'An error'], 0, 'Without return a function gives back None, and print shows the word None.'),
  ('quick', 'def sq(n): return n * n. What does print(sq(3) + sq(4)) show?', ['25', '7', '49', 'None'], 0, '9 + 16 = 25. Each call returns a number, so the + adds them.'),
  ('quick', 'x = 5 is outside. A function does x = 8. After you call the function, what is x outside?', ['5', '8', 'None', 'Error'], 0, 'The function made its own local x. The outside x is a different variable and stays 5.'),
  ])
